{"name":"Beach Wind","hex":"#dce1e2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"beach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The shore of a body of water, especially when sandy or pebbly.","A horizontal strip of land, usually sandy, adjoining water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To run aground on a beach.","To run (something) aground on a beach."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beach"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beach","blocks":[{"text":"A beach is a landform alongside a body of water which consists of loose particles. The particles composing a beach are typically made from rock, such as sand, gravel, shingle, pebbles, etc., or biological sources, such as mollusc shells or coralline algae. Sediments settle in different densities and structures, depending on the local wave action and weather, creating different textures, colors and gradients or layers of material."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Beach_at_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg/330px-Beach_at_Fort_Lauderdale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beach","wikidata":"Q40080"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg/500px-Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg/1280px-Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg/500px-Lago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALago_di_Lavarone_190820_03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Man_o%27war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg/500px-Man_o%27war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Man_o%27war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg/1280px-Man_o%27war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Man_o%27war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg/500px-Man_o%27war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMan_o'war_cove_near_lulworth_dorset_arp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Beach_zones.png/500px-Beach_zones.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Beach_zones.png/500px-Beach_zones.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A berm is a nearly horizontal portion that stays dry except during extremely high tides and storms. The swash zone is alternately covered and exposed by wave run-up. The beach face is the sloping section below the berm that is exposed to the swash of the waves. The wrack line (not shown here) is the highest reach of the daily tide where organic and inorganic debris is deposited by wave action. May have sand dunes.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeach_zones.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Summer_versus_winter_beach.png/500px-Summer_versus_winter_beach.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Summer_versus_winter_beach.png/500px-Summer_versus_winter_beach.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The differences between summer and winter on beaches in areas where the winter conditions are rougher and waves have a shorter wavelength but higher energy. In winter, sand from the beach is stored offshore.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASummer_versus_winter_beach.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Scala_dei_Turchi_Sand_062313.jpg/500px-Scala_dei_Turchi_Sand_062313.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Scala_dei_Turchi_Sand_062313.jpg/1280px-Scala_dei_Turchi_Sand_062313.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Scala_dei_Turchi_Sand_062313.jpg/500px-Scala_dei_Turchi_Sand_